WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.769
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.769 --> 00:00:09.880
As humans, when looking at the world, our
eyes and brain does not process the entirety

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:15.000
of the image we have in front of us, but plays
an interesting trick on us. We can only see

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:21.380
fine details in a tiny, tiny foveated region
that we are gazing at, while our peripheral

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:26.800
or indirect vision only sees a sparse, blurry
version of the image, and the rest of the

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.660
information is filled in by our brain. This
is a very efficient system, because our vision

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:37.790
system only has to process a tiny fraction
of the visual data that is in front of us,

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:43.660
and it still enables us to interact with the
world around us. So what if we would take

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:49.890
a learning algorithm that does something similar
for digital videos? Imagine that we would

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:56.530
need to render a sparse video only every tenth
pixel filled with information, and some kind

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:01.750
of neural network-based technique would be
able to reconstruct the full image similarly

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:07.819
to what our brain does. Yes, but that is very
little information to reconstruct an image

00:01:07.819 --> 00:01:10.450
from. So, is it possible?

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:15.799
Well, hold on to your papers, because this
new work can reconstruct a near-perfect image

00:01:15.799 --> 00:01:22.720
by looking at less than 10% of the input pixels.
So we have this as an input, and we get this.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:29.719
Wow. What is happening here is called a neural
reconstruction of foveated rendering data,

00:01:29.719 --> 00:01:35.560
or you are welcome to refer to it as foveated
reconstruction in short during your conversations

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:40.799
over dinner. The scrambled text part here
is quite interesting, one might think that,

00:01:40.799 --> 00:01:45.999
well, it could be better, however, given the
fact that if you look at the appropriate place

00:01:45.999 --> 00:01:51.549
in the sparse image, I not only cannot read
the text, I am not even sure if I see anything

00:01:51.549 --> 00:01:54.689
that indicates that there is text there at
all!

00:01:54.689 --> 00:01:59.490
So far, the example assumed that we are looking
at a particular point in the middle of the

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:04.569
screen, and the ultimate question is, how
does this deal with a real-life case where

00:02:04.569 --> 00:02:13.019
the user is looking around? Let’s see! This
is the input….and the reconstruction. Witchcraft.

00:02:13.019 --> 00:02:18.569
Let’s have a look at some more results.
Note that this method is developed for head-mounted

00:02:18.569 --> 00:02:23.361
displays, where we have information on where
the user is looking over time, and this can

00:02:23.361 --> 00:02:27.710
make all the difference in terms of optimization.

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:32.310
You see a comparison here against a method
labeled as “Multiresolution”, this is

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:37.680
from a paper by the name “Foveated 3D Graphics”,
and you can see that the difference in the

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.180
quality of the reconstruction is truly remarkable.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:47.599
Additionally, it has been trained on 350 thousand
short natural video sequences, and the whole

00:02:47.599 --> 00:02:53.560
thing runs in real time! Also, note that we
often discuss image inpainting methods in

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:59.180
this series, for instance, what you see here
is the legendary PatchMatch algorithm that

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:04.870
is one of these, and it is able to fill in
missing parts of an image. However, in image

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:10.379
inpainting, most of the image is intact, with
smaller regions that are missing. This is

00:03:10.379 --> 00:03:14.620
even more difficult than image inpainting,
because the vast majority of the image is

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:20.340
completely missing. The fact that we can now
do this with learning-based methods is absolutely

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.430
incredible.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:27.389
The first author of the paper is Anton Kaplanyan,
who is a brilliant and very rigorous mathematician,

00:03:27.389 --> 00:03:32.959
so of course, the results are evaluated in
detail, both in terms of mathematics, and

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:36.939
with a user study. Make sure to have a look
at the paper for more on that!

00:03:36.939 --> 00:03:41.579
We got to know each other with Anton during
the days when all we did was light transport

00:03:41.579 --> 00:03:47.069
simulations, all day, every day, and were
always speculating about potential projects,

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:52.359
and to my great sadness, somehow, unfortunately
we never managed to work together for a full

00:03:52.359 --> 00:03:58.450
project. Again, congratulations Anton! Stunning,
beautiful work. What a time to be alive!

